= Boids =

'''Boids''' is Craig Reynolds's 1987 computer model for simulating flocking-like collective motion using locally interacting virtual agents. Each simulated individual—called a '''boid'''—steers using information about nearby boids rather than following a central leader or a precomputed global trajectory.

Reynolds introduced the model for computer graphics to create realistic-looking flocks, herds, and schools while avoiding explicit scripting of every group movement.<ref name="reynolds">[https://doi.org/10.1145/37401.37406 Reynolds, C. W. (1987), “Flocks, Herds, and Schools: A Distributed Behavioral Model,” ''Computer Graphics'' 21(4), 25–34.]</ref>

The model is important in [[Swarm intelligence]], [[Emergence]], artificial life, and animation because simple local steering can generate coherent group patterns. It is not a complete model of real bird, fish, or herd behavior.

== The three commonly summarized rules ==

Boids is commonly explained through three local steering tendencies.

'''Separation''' — steer away from nearby agents to avoid crowding.

'''Alignment''' — steer toward the average heading or velocity of nearby agents.

'''Cohesion''' — steer toward the local center of nearby agents.

The original Reynolds system also included broader steering and environmental behaviors, but these three rules became the canonical minimal description.

== Separation ==

Separation produces short-range repulsion.

Without separation, agents can collapse onto the same position.

The rule is often weighted more strongly for closer neighbors.

This creates a local exclusion region while still allowing the flock to remain cohesive.

The exact force law is an implementation choice rather than a fixed part of the conceptual model.

== Alignment ==

Alignment adjusts one boid's velocity toward the average velocity of its neighbors.

This produces directional coordination.

If alignment is too strong, the flock can become rigid.

If too weak, individuals can move incoherently.

Alignment depends on which neighbors are visible to the agent.

== Cohesion ==

Cohesion moves a boid toward the local center of neighboring positions.

It prevents indefinite dispersion.

Cohesion competes with separation.

The flock remains together because attraction operates at larger local distances while repulsion dominates at short distances.

== Local perception ==

A boid normally responds to a neighborhood rather than the whole population.

Neighborhoods can be defined by:

* fixed metric radius;
* nearest <math>k</math> neighbors;
* field of view;
* topological adjacency.

This choice changes the dynamics.

A metric neighborhood can contain many agents at high density and few at low density.

A fixed-neighbor neighborhood keeps information load more stable.

== No central leader ==

The canonical boids model does not require one leader directing the group.

Every boid applies similar local rules.

Global motion emerges from their coupled updates.

A leader can be added as an extension, but leadership is not required for basic flock formation.

== Emergence ==

Boids is a standard example of [[Emergence]].

The programmer specifies local rules.

The programmer does not explicitly script:

* every flock shape;
* every turn;
* every local split;
* every rejoining event.

These macro-patterns arise from repeated local interaction.

“Emergent” does not mean unexplained; the steering rules are the mechanism.

== Self-organization ==

The flock is also an example of [[Self-organization]] because coordinated structure forms without central control.

Self-organization can fail.

Poorly chosen parameters can produce:

* dispersal;
* oscillation;
* collision;
* unstable clusters.

The existence of local rules does not guarantee useful organization.

== Time stepping ==

A simulation repeatedly:

# computes neighbors;
# computes steering;
# updates velocity;
# updates position.

Update order matters.

If one boid is updated and immediately affects the next boid in the same time step, results can depend on loop order.

Many implementations compute all steering from a snapshot and then update all agents together.

== Speed limits ==

Boids often use maximum speed.

Without a speed cap, repeated steering can accelerate agents indefinitely.

Similarly, maximum acceleration or steering force can make turns smoother and physically more plausible.

These limits are simulation parameters, not biological laws.

== Boundary conditions ==

The simulation world needs boundary rules.

Common choices include:

* wraparound torus;
* reflective walls;
* steering away from boundaries;
* open space.

Boundary choice can create artificial patterns.

A torus removes walls but allows agents leaving one side to reappear on the opposite side.

Results should report the boundary model.

== Obstacles ==

Obstacle avoidance can be added as another local steering behavior.

A boid can sense predicted collisions and steer around objects.

Combining obstacle avoidance with flocking requires priority or weighting.

If cohesion overwhelms obstacle avoidance, collisions occur.

If avoidance dominates, the flock can fragment.

== Goals and path following ==

Reynolds later generalized steering behaviors to include:

* seek;
* flee;
* pursue;
* evade;
* wander;
* path following;
* obstacle avoidance.

These behaviors can be combined with flocking.

Boids should therefore be understood as part of a broader steering-behavior framework.

== Neighborhood search ==

A naive implementation checks every pair of boids.

For <math>n</math> agents, this costs approximately:

<math>O(n^2)</math>

neighbor comparisons per step.

This becomes expensive for large populations.

Spatial indexing can reduce candidate neighbor checks.

== Spatial grids ==

A uniform grid partitions space into cells.

A boid only checks its own cell and nearby cells.

When interaction radius and density are bounded, this can reduce practical cost toward linear scaling with population.

Performance depends on cell size and spatial distribution.

== Spatial trees ==

Quadtrees, octrees, or related spatial trees can organize agents hierarchically.

They are useful when density is nonuniform.

Tree construction and query overhead can outweigh gains for small swarms.

The data structure is an engineering choice.

== Quantifying flocking ==

Visual appearance is subjective.

Useful quantitative measures include:

* polarization;
* nearest-neighbor distance;
* collision rate;
* group diameter;
* number of connected components;
* velocity variance.

Metrics allow parameter comparisons and reproducible evaluation.

== Polarization ==

Polarization measures directional alignment.

If each boid has unit heading <math>\hat v_i</math>:

<math>\Phi=\frac{1}{n}\left\|\sum_i\hat v_i\right\|.</math>

<math>\Phi=1</math> indicates perfect alignment.

Values near zero indicate disordered headings.

High polarization does not necessarily mean the flock is spatially cohesive.

== Cohesion metrics ==

Spatial cohesion can be measured by:

* mean distance to centroid;
* maximum diameter;
* cluster count.

A flock can be highly aligned while split into distant subgroups.

Multiple metrics are therefore preferable.

== Connectivity ==

Define a graph where boids are connected when within interaction range.

[[Connectivity (graph theory)]] then measures whether information could propagate through local neighborhoods.

A flock can fragment when the interaction graph disconnects.

This gives a precise bridge between geometry and collective dynamics.

== Noise ==

Noise can be added to:

* heading;
* position;
* sensing;
* steering.

Small noise can produce natural-looking variation.

Large noise can destroy coherent flocking.

Studying the order-disorder transition connects boids-like models with statistical-physics flocking models.

== Reynolds boids versus Vicsek model ==

The Vicsek model is a separate minimal model of collective motion emphasizing noisy alignment at constant speed.

Boids combines several steering tendencies and originated in computer graphics.

The models should not be conflated.

The Vicsek model is often more suitable for analytical statistical-physics questions.

== Biological flocking ==

Real animals use:

* vision;
* hearing;
* sensory delay;
* aerodynamic or hydrodynamic interactions;
* individual preferences;
* anti-predator behavior.

Real bird flocking can depend on a fixed number of nearest neighbors rather than a simple metric radius in some empirical studies.

Therefore the boids rules are abstractions.

== Swarm intelligence ==

[[Swarm intelligence]] is broader.

It includes optimization, insect-inspired coordination, robotics, and other decentralized systems.

Boids is specifically a model of collective motion.

It is not a general-purpose optimization method.

== Emergent algorithms ==

Boids can be described as an [[Emergent algorithm]] because local update rules generate a global pattern.

However, the term emergent algorithm is informal.

The precise boids model is defined by its state variables, neighborhood rule, steering functions, and update schedule.

== Artificial life ==

Boids influenced [[Artificial life]] by showing how life-like collective behavior could emerge from simple simulated agents.

Life-like appearance should not be confused with biological life.

Boids do not reproduce, metabolize, evolve, or possess cells.

== Computer animation ==

The original goal was animation.

Distributed control allowed groups to turn and avoid obstacles without manually scripting every individual trajectory.

This made large groups easier to animate.

Modern crowd and flock simulations often use more complex steering, physics, and planning.

== Reproducibility ==

A boids experiment should report:

* number of agents;
* initial positions;
* initial velocities;
* perception radius;
* field of view;
* rule weights;
* acceleration limit;
* speed limit;
* timestep;
* boundary conditions;
* random seed.

Without these parameters, two “boids” simulations can behave very differently.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Boids demonstrates that local rules are sufficient to generate certain flock-like visual and quantitative patterns.

It does not prove that real animals use exactly those rules.

Matching appearance is weaker than matching measured trajectories, neighbor relations, sensory constraints, and perturbation responses.

